    fact: [
      ["fact_mult_symbols", "Multiplication can be written many ways", "× · ∗", "Multiplication is sometimes written ×, sometimes as a dot ·, sometimes as an asterisk * (on computers), and sometimes with nothing at all: 3x means 3 × x and (2)(5) means 2 × 5.", { ins: "×", pre: ["mul"] }],
      ["fact_div_symbols", "Division can be written many ways", "÷ / ─", "Division can be ÷, a slash /, or a fraction bar with one number over the other. 6 ÷ 2, 6/2 and the fraction six over two all mean 3.", { ins: "÷", pre: ["div"] }],
      ["fact_div_zero", "You can’t divide by zero", "x ÷ 0", "12 ÷ 3 = 4 because 4 × 3 = 12. For 12 ÷ 0 we would need a number that times 0 is 12, and none exists. So division by zero is undefined.", { pre: ["div"] }],
      ["fact_zero_even", "Zero is an even number", "0", "Zero splits into two equal groups of nothing with nothing left over, and it sits between the odd numbers −1 and 1.", { pre: ["evenodd"] }],
      ["fact_one_prime", "One is not a prime number", "1", "Primes need exactly two divisors. 1 has only one (itself). Leaving 1 out keeps prime factorizations unique.", { pre: ["prime"] }],
      ["fact_two_prime", "Two is the only even prime", "2", "Every other even number can be divided by 2, so it has at least three divisors.", { pre: ["prime"] }],
      ["fact_point_nine", "0.999… equals 1", "0.9̅ = 1", "1/3 = 0.333…, so 3 × 1/3 = 0.999… = 1. There is no number between them, so they are the same number.", { pre: ["decimal", "frac"] }],
      ["fact_power_zero", "Anything to the power 0 is 1", "x⁰ = 1", "2³ = 8, 2² = 4, 2¹ = 2: each step divides by 2. One more step gives 2⁰ = 1. (0⁰ is usually taken as 1 too.)", { pre: ["pow"] }],
      ["fact_zero_fact", "0! equals 1", "0! = 1", "There is exactly one way to arrange nothing: do nothing. It also keeps n! = n × (n − 1)! working for n = 1.", { pre: ["fact"] }],
      ["fact_neg_neg", "Negative times negative is positive", "(−)(−) = +", "−3 × 2 = −6 and each time the 2 drops by 1, the answer goes up by 3: −3 × 0 = 0, so −3 × −1 = 3.", { pre: ["neg", "mul"] }],
      ["fact_order", "Order of operations (PEMDAS / BODMAS)", "( ) ^ × ÷ + −", "Brackets first, then powers, then multiplication and division (left to right), then addition and subtraction (left to right). 2 + 3 × 4 = 14, not 20.", { pre: ["paren"] }],
      ["fact_pi_irrational", "π never ends or repeats", "π", "π is irrational: it can’t be written as a fraction, and its decimal digits never settle into a pattern. Over 100 trillion digits have been computed.", { pre: ["pi", "irrational"] }],
      ["fact_sqrt2", "√2 is irrational", "√2", "Ancient Greeks proved √2 can’t be a fraction. Legend says Hippasus was thrown overboard for revealing it.", { pre: ["sqrt", "irrational"] }],
      ["fact_triangle_angles", "A triangle’s angles add up to 180°", "∠A + ∠B + ∠C", "Tear off the three corners of any paper triangle and they line up into a straight line.", { pre: ["angle", "triangle"] }],
      ["fact_circle_360", "A full turn is 360°", "360°", "The Babylonians used 360, perhaps because a year has about 360 days and 360 divides evenly by many numbers.", { pre: ["angle"] }],
      ["fact_equals_history", "The = sign was invented in 1557", "=", "Robert Recorde got tired of writing ‘is equal to’ and drew two parallel lines.", { pre: ["eq"] }],
      ["fact_zero_history", "Zero was invented as a number in India", "0", "Brahmagupta wrote the rules for zero as a number around 628 AD. Roman numerals have no zero.", { pre: ["zero"] }],
      ["fact_111", "111,111,111² = 12,345,678,987,654,321", "1²=1, 11²=121", "Squaring repunits makes palindromes that count up and down, until the digits start carrying.", { pre: ["sq", "repunit"] }],
      ["fact_gauss", "1 + 2 + … + 100 = 5050", "5050", "Young Gauss paired 1 + 100, 2 + 99, …: 50 pairs of 101.", { pre: ["triangular"] }],
      ["fact_euler_identity", "e^(iπ) + 1 = 0", "e^(iπ) + 1 = 0", "Euler’s identity links five key numbers, 0, 1, π, e and i, in one short equation.", { pre: ["euler", "pi", "imag"] }],
      ["fact_algebra_word", "‘Algebra’ comes from Arabic", "al-jabr", "From the title of al-Khwārizmī’s 9th-century book. ‘Algorithm’ comes from his name.", { pre: ["algebra"] }],
      ["fact_commutative", "You can swap the order in + and ×", "a+b = b+a", "Addition and multiplication are commutative: 3 × 5 = 5 × 3. Subtraction and division are not.", { pre: ["add", "mul"] }],
      ["fact_googol", "A googol is 10¹⁰⁰", "10¹⁰⁰", "A 1 followed by 100 zeros, named by a nine-year-old. A googolplex is 10^googol. The company Google is named after it.", { pre: ["sci"] }],
      ["fact_graham", "Graham’s number is unimaginably big", "G", "It was once the largest number used in a serious proof. Writing its digits would not fit in the observable universe, yet we know it ends in …7262464195387.", { pre: ["pow"] }],
      ["fact_base10", "We count in tens because of fingers", "10", "Most cultures count in tens, but the Babylonians used 60 (hence 60 minutes) and the Maya used 20.", { pre: ["base"] }],
      ["fact_decimal_comma", "Some countries write decimals with a comma", "3,14", "In much of Europe, 3,14 means 3.14 and 1.000 means one thousand.", { pre: ["decimal"] }],
      ["fact_infinities", "Some infinities are bigger than others", "ℵ₀ < 𝔠", "There are more real numbers than whole numbers, as Cantor proved in 1891.", { pre: ["cantor"] }],
      ["fact_squares_odd", "Square numbers are sums of odd numbers", "1+3+5 = 9", "1 = 1, 1 + 3 = 4, 1 + 3 + 5 = 9, 1 + 3 + 5 + 7 = 16. Each odd number adds an L-shaped layer to a square.", { pre: ["square_nums"] }],
      ["fact_nine_trick", "The nines times-table trick", "9 × n", "In 9 × n (n from 1 to 10), the digits of the answer add to 9, and the tens digit is n − 1: 9 × 7 = 63.", { pre: ["mul"] }],
      ["fact_percent_swap", "Percentages can be swapped", "8% of 50 = 50% of 8", "x% of y is always equal to y% of x. 4% of 75 is the same as 75% of 4, which is 3.", { pre: ["pct"] }],
      ["fact_prime_6k", "Primes above 3 are next to a multiple of 6", "6k ± 1", "Every prime bigger than 3 is one more or one less than a multiple of 6: 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, …", { pre: ["prime"] }],
      ["fact_handshakes", "n people make n(n−1)/2 handshakes", "n(n−1)/2", "If everyone shakes hands once with everyone else, 10 people make 45 handshakes.", { pre: ["choose"] }],
      ["fact_x_variable", "Letters can stand for numbers", "x", "In algebra a letter such as x holds a number we don’t know yet. Descartes started using x, y, z for unknowns.", { pre: ["variable"] }],
      ["fact_minus_two_meanings", "The minus sign has two jobs", "−", "It means ‘subtract’ in 7 − 2, and ‘negative’ in −2. Some calculators have a separate (−) key for negatives.", { pre: ["sub", "neg"] }],
      ["fact_mean_median", "Mean and median can be very different", "x̄ vs median", "Incomes 1, 2, 3, 4, 90: the mean is 20 but the median is 3. One huge value pulls the mean.", { pre: ["mean"] }]
    ],
